WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:13.010
Nuclear power is like adult entertainment…
a lot of people say they don’t like it but,

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.080
then, you find it all over the place.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.890
Maybe you thought nuclear power wasn’t fashionable
anymore.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.750
Maybe you thought that after the Fukushima
disaster, the whole world was showing its

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.460
back to nuclear power.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.700
Well… the opposite is true.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:34.940
In fact, if you’re watching this video from
America, the UK, or any European country,

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.390
chances are, you’re consuming nuclear power
at this very moment.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.070
And I know what you’re gonna say now…

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.070
Come on, Simon!

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.070
You’re exaggerating!

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.829
It’s not like most countries have nuclear
power plants.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:46.829
Right?

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:49.899
In fact, there are only 30 countries with
nuclear reactors.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:53.760
This means, just one out of 6 or seven countries
have it.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.480
But think about it… many of those countries
are really small.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.609
And, as you can imagine, VANUATU doesn’t
have the same power needs as CHINA.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:08.790
If we’re talking about the biggest economies,
those with membership in the G20, only 5 of

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.360
them have no reactors.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.830
And of those, Turkey is starting to build
their own facilities and Italy imports 10%

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.970
of their energy from the nuclear plants in
France.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.100
So let’s face it… most wealthy countries
use nuclear power.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.330
And the emerging ones… are starting to join
the club too.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.330
Especially China.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.490
You see, there are three indicators for determining
whether a country will adopt nuclear power

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.060
that go hand in hand.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.230
Economic growth, more power demand, and more
pollution.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.820
So imagine for a moment Chinese factories,
demanding more and more energy.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.130
Add to this the heavy migration from villages
to cities and…

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.130
Boom!

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:50.540
All of a sudden we have thousands of offices,
factories, malls and millions of Chinese citizens

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:57.590
turning on their lights at the same time.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.550
Cities like Beijing, Shanghai and Shenzhen
make most American cities look like a small

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.310
village in the Alps.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:06.900
So imagine how much energy they need.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.899
Of course, this explains why China is becoming
a green energy Disneyland.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:15.860
They can’t stop building solar panels, wind
turbines and hydroelectric plants.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.840
Year after year, China is leading investment
in renewable energy sources.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.319
And you would think that, after Fukushima,
they’re no longer interested in nuclear,

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:25.319
right?

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:28.080
Well… again, wrong.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.629
Look, according to the International Atomic
Energy Agency, Asia is where half of all new

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:34.629
reactors are being built.

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:40.290
And we’re not talking about 3 or 4… in
total, more than 27 reactors are currently

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.950
being built, and most of them are in China.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.609
And I know what you’re thinking.

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:49.659
Well, China’s a dictatorship, right?

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:54.450
So the only reason they’re building so many
reactors is because they don’t need to listen

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.450
to their people.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.670
They can do whatever they want.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:58.890
Again, wrong.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.629
Asian citizens are largely in support of nuclear
power.

00:03:01.629 --> 00:03:04.739
Let’s look at Taiwan, for example-- a democratic
country.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:10.569
Here, they made a referendum to close their
reactors and their citizens said no.

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:15.769
Even in Japan, the land of Fukushima, we’re
seeing news like this.

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:20.950
Japan court rejects lawsuit against construction
of nuclear plant

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.639
So the question is… why?

00:03:22.639 --> 00:03:27.849
What happened with nuclear power that Asians
and, particularly, the Chinese, are so excited

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:28.849
about?

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:30.159
What are the main companies involved in this?

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:32.079
Is it a good or a bad idea?

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:35.950
Today we are going to answer all of these
questions but, before we do, let’s take

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.609
a look back at the history.

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:42.950
THE SLEEPING DRAGON
Do you remember the dragon, Smaug, from The

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.409
Hobbit, sleeping on top of a mountain of gold?

00:03:45.409 --> 00:03:48.439
Well… in Mao Zedong’s China, Mao was that
sleeping dragon.

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:53.189
But, instead of sleeping on wealth and treasure,
he was sleeping on rice.

00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:57.540
China was poor and agricultural, and this
how it remained for decades.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.139
During those times, China was too poor to
buy the insanely pricey technology needed

00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:02.319
for nuclear plants.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:06.769
At the beginning of the 90s, China had no
reactors whatsoever.

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:14.480
In 1990, the power mix in this country was
20% hydroelectric and 80% fossil fuels, mainly

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.019
coal.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:21.450
But then, in 2017, things changed.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.550
Of course, China still relies on its coal.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.280
But not as much.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.280
Why?

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.370
Look, coal produces the most pollution of
any energy source.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:36.080
This explains why Beijing is so famous for
its smog and for people having to wear masks.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.880
And don’t get me wrong, this pollution won’t
change in the near future.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.680
Especially with news like this.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.610
China coal power building boom sparks climate
warning

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.009
Think about it: the only natural resource
China has is coal.

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:54.020
So they can’t stop using it just like that.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.300
But there is a problem.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.530
Coal and hydroelectric produced enough power
during Mao’s time.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:03.830
But in just 20 years, energy consumption in
China has multiplied by 10.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:12.900
So they need other sources of energy badly.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.740
And this is why, in just 27 years, China has
built 40 brand new reactors.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.930
That’s already more than Russia has.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.860
So we could say that the sleeping dragon is
now wide awake.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:28.060
But, OK, OK, we said that China is investing
a lot in green energy so… why do they still

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.289
need nuclear?

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:34.210
Let’s have a look!

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.580
HUNGRY FOR URANIUM
Smaug the dragon, loved gold.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.940
The chinese dragon loves uranium.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.330
Right now, China is building 11 brand new
reactors with a greater capacity than all

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.530
of the United Kingdom’s reactors.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:53.569
But wait a minute because this is nothing…
just look at the expectations for 2030!

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:58.750
China set to overtake U.S. as biggest nuclear
energy nation, IEA says

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:06.319
I know what you’re thinking.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:12.020
All of these predictions are after the Fukushima
incident in 2011, which was caused by a tsunami

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:17.120
that hit a nuclear power plant in Japan: a
huge catastrophe, forcing thousands of people

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.289
to move, turning the water toxic, and making
death a real and present danger.

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:26.520
So… why does China keep investing in nuclear?

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.490
Isn’t their green energy enough?

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:37.569
Well… here’s the important thing… the
key point when we’re talking about energy

00:06:37.569 --> 00:06:40.400
is that… hardly any of it can be stored!

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.080
Maybe in the future, lithium batteries will
improve, but currently, it is very expensive

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.490
to store electricity on a large scale.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:54.349
And this is the main issue with green energy.

00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:56.780
Solar panels are good but… what happens
at night?

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:00.289
Wind turbines are great but… what do we
do when there is no wind?

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:05.719
Add to this what we said before: so far, China
relies too much on coal because they don’t

00:07:05.719 --> 00:07:06.919
have to import it.

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:09.360
But this causes a lot of pollution.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.050
So what are the alternatives?

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:14.530
Well, what about gas?

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.419
Right now, China imports 40% of the gas they
use.

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:20.070
And you know all the political problems this
creates.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.750
We have a whole series of videos covering
the gas wars.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:27.970
Besides, gas in Asia is very expensive because
of things like this.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:35.150
Japan spot LNG price hits 3-year high in Jan
Basically, China is constantly competing with

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:39.409
Japan to attract all those ships with liquified
natural gas.

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:41.840
And this has increased the price a lot.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.870
So there’s only one alternative: NUCLEAR.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:49.819
It’s efficient, people can use it all day
long and, as counter intuitive as it sounds,

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:51.540
it is less polluting.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.650
But wait a minute because it’s not that
simple!

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:59.439
You see, if you wanna have nukes, you need
two things: some very expensive technology

00:07:59.439 --> 00:08:01.360
and… uranium!

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.800
And uranium isn’t something you can go buy
at the supermarket.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.229
So how is china doing it?

00:08:07.229 --> 00:08:12.659
We’ll tell you right now.

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:15.860
MADE IN CHINA
China is famous for copying products from

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:16.860
the West.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.689
So yes, you can imagine what we’re gonna
say now.

00:08:19.689 --> 00:08:24.169
What did China do to import nuclear technology?

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.319
Learn from the best!

00:08:25.319 --> 00:08:28.949
In the beginning, they started working with
the French nuclear behemoth, FRAMATOME, which

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:30.800
you might know by their old name, AREVA.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.260
They also made deals with the American Westinghouse
and the Russian Rosatom.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.640
This was during the 90s.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.649
But what happened in the 2000s?

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:48.350
Well, China went to all these companies and
said ‘OK, guys, from now on, next to any

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:51.389
of your engineers, there must be a Chinese
engineer’.

00:08:51.389 --> 00:08:56.649
This is how China gained all of the technological
secrets from the best nuclear companies.

00:08:56.649 --> 00:09:02.100
Yes, you’re right… nobody negotiates better
than the Chinese.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:09.590
So, after 10 years, the third generation of
Chinese manufactured nuclear reactors is already

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.180
here!

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:14.970
China Set to Fire Up World’s First AP1000
Nuclear Reactor

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.170
Yes, now China can build its own reactors
without the help of foreign companies.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:23.610
And, even if the American Westinghouse wanted
to expand their presence in the Chinese market,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:30.250
they would have a really hard time because
of news like this.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:34.070
Westinghouse Could Be Hurt by New U.S. Curbs
on Nuclear Exports

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:36.810
But still, there is one thing you cannot copy.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:38.820
And this is URANIUM.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.270
Nuclear power plants need uranium as fuel.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:44.450
And China does not naturally have this resource.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:49.810
So they must import it.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:53.490
But, again China is great at making deals.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.210
The world’s main uranium producer is Kazachstan.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:58.779
They produce 40% of all uranium on the planet.

00:09:58.779 --> 00:10:01.330
And China already takes 50% of that.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:05.089
They also have some agreements with Canada
and they’re trying to do the same with Australia.

00:10:05.089 --> 00:10:06.089
But…

00:10:06.089 --> 00:10:07.360
OK, this is the easy part.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.139
What makes China so special is their ability
to make deals even in the most challenging

00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:16.209
places…

00:10:16.209 --> 00:10:24.639
China buys Rio Tinto’s stake in Namibia
uranium for $107 million

00:10:24.639 --> 00:10:28.740
China gains ground in Niger to get uranium
to increase their nuclear production

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.310
So, in summary, “made in China” has expanded
to the whole World.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:33.880
But not only that!

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.931
China’s affordable technology is making
nuclear power available in places where it

00:10:37.931 --> 00:10:39.600
was impossible to imagine before.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.700
For example, the crown jewel of this brand
new industry, the HUALONG ONE reactor, will

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:48.410
be used in countries like ARGENTINA and PAKISTAN,
which are going to start using more and more

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:49.870
nuclear power too.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.269
But this isn’t just about the emerging world!

00:10:52.269 --> 00:10:55.820
The state owned Chinese nuclear company is
participating in the construction of a brand

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.870
new reactor in the United Kingdom.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.050
But this is a story for another video.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:03.680
Because, as you might have already guessed,
this is just the first chapter of another

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.590
series of videos we’re going to release
about the comeback of nuclear power.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.200
If you don’t wanna miss this, don’t forget
to subscribe and hit the bell button, so you

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:12.500
won’t miss any posts.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.370
In the meantime, now it’s your turn…

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:21.820
Where do you think these Chinese nuclear ambitions
will lead?

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.050
Do you think they’ll keep building nuclear
plants, or will they focus most of their efforts

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.590
into developing more green energy?

